Good 👍 since you’re using a container as your form area, disabling it works a bit differently in Power Apps.

Containers don’t truly “disable” like forms—but we can simulate disable behavior perfectly 👇


---

🎯 GOAL

If varHasAccess = false
→ User CANNOT click radio buttons
→ UI looks disabled


---

🧩 OPTION 1: DISABLE ALL INPUT CONTROLS (RECOMMENDED ✅)

👉 You must control the DisplayMode of the controls inside the container


---

For your Radio buttons (Radio_Score_New)

Set:

DisplayMode = If(varHasAccess, DisplayMode.Edit, DisplayMode.Disabled)


---

👉 Do this for:

Radio buttons ✅ (most important)

Text inputs (if any)

Dropdowns (if any)



---

🧠 WHY THIS IS REQUIRED

Container → cannot block interaction ❌
Controls → must be disabled individually ✅


---

🧩 OPTION 2: OVERLAY (BEST UI EXPERIENCE ⭐)

👉 This is the cleanest and most professional approach


---

Step 1: Insert a rectangle ON TOP of your container

Rename:

Overlay_Disable


---

Step 2: Set properties

Visible = !varHasAccess
Fill = RGBA(255,255,255,0.6)


---

👉 This creates: ✔ semi-transparent layer
✔ blocks all clicks
✔ looks disabled


---

Step 3: Add message (optional)

Insert label ON TOP of overlay:

Text = "You are not authorized to access this section"
Visible = !varHasAccess
Align = Align.Center


---

🧩 OPTION 3: DISABLE ENTIRE CONTAINER (LIMITED)

You can try:

DisplayMode = If(varHasAccess, DisplayMode.Edit, DisplayMode.Disabled)

⚠️ BUT:

Not all controls respect it

Nested galleries may ignore it


👉 So not reliable ❌


---

🧩 BEST PRACTICE (COMBINE)

👉 Use BOTH:

✔ Disable controls

Radio.DisplayMode = If(varHasAccess, DisplayMode.Edit, DisplayMode.Disabled)

✔ Overlay

Overlay.Visible = !varHasAccess


